└── synchronous HTTP client for polling REST endpoints
    ├── Candidates : requests, httpx
    ├── Used APIs  : Session, session.get, resp.raise_for_status, resp.json
    ├── Pitfalls   : requests.Session() vs httpx.Client() have different constructor semantics (e.g. connection pooling, default timeouts); timeout semantics 
    │   differ: requests uses per-request float/tuple timeouts, httpx uses httpx.Timeout objects with more granular control; raised exception types differ: 
    │   requests.exceptions.HTTPError vs httpx.HTTPStatusError/httpx.HTTPError; response.json() behavior on malformed JSON may raise different exception types 
    │   between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── requests.Session is used to perform synchronous GET requests with timeout and JSON parsing. httpx offers an httpx.Client with a near-identical 
        synchronous API (get, raise_for_status, json) and can be a drop-in replacement for sync workflows. urllib3 was suggested but is a lower-level 
        dependency that requests itself is built on (no Session/JSON convenience layer), so it's excluded per the dependency-chain rule. aiohttp was suggested 
        but is async-only, requiring the entire ScadaPoller class (read_tag/poll) to be rewritten with async/await, which is a much deeper architectural change
        than a simple client swap, so it's excluded here.

