└── Modbus TCP client for reading holding registers from a power meter
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus, modbus-tk, umodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, result.isError, result.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns a response object with .isError() and .registers; minimalmodbus raises exceptions on error and returns raw register lists
    │   directly, requiring rewritten error handling; minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RS-485/RS-232) communication; TCP support is limited/less
    │   idiomatic compared to pymodbus; modbus-tk uses a master/hub-based API (execute() with function codes) rather than named methods like 
    │   read_holding_registers, requiring significant call-site rewrites; umodbus's client API is lower-level, requiring manual PDU/ADU construction 
    │   (modbus.client.tcp.read_holding_registers + send_message), which differs substantially from pymodbus's object-oriented client; unit/slave ID parameter 
    │   naming and semantics differ across libraries (unit vs slaveaddress vs slave_id); connection lifecycle management (connect/close, socket vs serial port 
    │   config) differs significantly between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four libraries implement the Modbus protocol and can act as a TCP/RTU client to read holding registers, making them functionally interchangeable at
        the protocol level. pymodbus is used here to instantiate a TCP client, connect, and read holding registers via a response-object API 
        (.isError()/.registers). minimalmodbus and modbus-tk also provide holding-register read functions (though minimalmodbus is serial-first and modbus-tk 
        uses a hub/master pattern with raw list returns), and umodbus exposes a lower-level client.tcp module for issuing Modbus TCP requests.

