    - paho's connect(host, port) takes separate host/port arguments and is synchronous; amqtt's MQTTClient.connect() takes a single URI string and must be 
awaited — the connection lifecycle model differs enough that wrapping alone changes program semantics (blocking-per-call event loop spin-up) rather than truly 
migrating it.
    - paho's publish() returns an object exposing .rc and .mid used directly via getattr(info, 'rc', 0); amqtt's publish() is an awaited coroutine with no 
equivalent return object, so the 'rc' semantics have no natural amqtt counterpart and would have to be synthesized/faked by a wrapper, not genuinely provided 
by amqtt.
    - TelemetryBridge's dependency-injected client design assumes a ready-to-use synchronous object at construction time; amqtt's client is meant to be used 
via async context manager / awaited connect, meaning the DI constructor itself would need to become async-aware — a structural redesign, not a call-site 
wrapper.

