Equivalence Groups Found
└── finite-state machine engine (dynamic model-based FSM)
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, automat, state_machine
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine(model=, states=, initial=), add_transition(trigger, source, dest, conditions=, after=)
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions binds behavior dynamically to an external `model` object (self) and resolves 'can_fill'/'open_inlet' etc. by string lookup at 
    │   runtime; python-statemachine/automat require the state machine logic to be defined declaratively on a class, forcing structural rewrites of 
    │   guard/action methods; automat uses decorator-based MethodicalMachine with @machine.input/@machine.output patterns entirely different from 
    │   add_transition() calls; wildcard transitions ('*' source in trip transition) are a transitions-specific feature not uniformly supported across 
    │   alternatives; conditions= and after= as string names resolved dynamically is a transitions-specific convention; other libraries use callables or method
    │   decorators instead
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions provides a dynamic, model-attaching FSM engine driven by add_transition() calls with conditions/after callbacks resolved by name on the 
        model instance. python-statemachine, automat, and state_machine all implement finite-state machines but with fundamentally different, class-declarative
        or decorator-based APIs (states/transitions declared as class attributes or via decorators rather than imperative add_transition calls bound to an 
        external model object). They are conceptually equivalent (FSM engines) but not drop-in API-compatible replacements.

