└── finite-state machine engine for process control (state transitions, guards, callbacks)
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, pysm, automat
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine, add_transition, conditions, after, model.state
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions uses an imperative API (Machine(model, states, initial) + add_transition calls) whereas python-statemachine and automat use 
    │   class-based declarative definitions (State() descriptors, @transition decorators) requiring the TankController class itself to be restructured, not 
    │   just import lines; pysm's Machine/State/Event API differs significantly from transitions' add_transition signature (conditions=, after=), so 
    │   guard/callback wiring must be manually re-implemented; automat's MethodicalMachine ties transitions to specially decorated instance methods, which 
    │   conflicts with transitions' dynamic model-attachment approach (self.machine = Machine(model=self, ...)); wildcard transitions ('*' source state used in
    │   trip()) are a transitions-specific feature not uniformly supported across alternatives; dynamically added methods/attributes (start_fill, 
    │   level_reached, etc.) generated by transitions at runtime must be manually recreated as explicit methods in declarative alternatives
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions provides a generic, imperative Machine object that is attached to a model and configured via add_transition() calls with conditions/after 
        callbacks referencing model methods. python-statemachine, pysm, and automat all provide finite-state-machine abstractions that could in principle 
        replace transitions for managing IDLE/FILLING/HOLDING/DRAINING/FAULT states, so they are functionally equivalent at the conceptual level 
        (declarative/programmatic FSM engines).

