└── Modbus TCP client for industrial field I/O (read/write holding registers)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient(host, port=port), client.connect(), client.read_holding_registers(address, count, unit=unit), rr.isError(), rr.registers, 
    │   client.write_register(address, value, unit=unit), wr.isError()
    ├── Pitfalls   : pyModbusTCP uses ModbusClient with methods like read_holding_registers() returning a list or None on error, not an object with 
    │   .isError()/.registers -- requires rewriting error handling; modbus-tk uses a Master object with execute() and modbus_tcp.TcpMaster, a completely 
    │   different call pattern (cst.READ_HOLDING_REGISTERS constants) than pymodbus's method-per-function-code API; unit/slave id parameter naming and defaults
    │   differ across libraries (unit= vs slave_id= vs no parameter); connection lifecycle methods differ (connect()/close() naming and auto-reconnect 
    │   behavior); exception vs error-flag-object error models differ, requiring changes to the IOError-raising logic in _read1 and write_valve
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk both implement Modbus TCP master/client functionality comparable to pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient, supporting reading/writing 
        holding registers over TCP -- the same functional role used in this file. minimalmodbus is excluded because it is designed for serial/RS-485 (RTU) 
        communication and does not support TCP transport, which is what this file explicitly requires (ModbusTcpClient with host/port). umodbus is excluded 
        because it is primarily oriented toward building Modbus servers/slaves (e.g. for embedded devices) rather than acting as a general-purpose TCP client, 
        which doesn't match the master/client read-write usage here.

